from __future__ import annotations

PRIVACY_STATEMENT = '# Glossa Lab — Privacy and Data-Handling Statement\n\n## 1. What this statement covers\n\nGlossa Lab is a research workbench: a program for testing claims\nabout the Indus script, claim by claim, against the evidence. It\ncomes in two editions that are two doors into the same program:\n\n- **The desktop edition** runs on one person\'s own computer. That\n  person is the operator, and everything the program holds sits on\n  that computer.\n- **The cloud edition**, at glossa.bitconcepts.tech, lets invited\n  members sign in through a website and use the same program, which\n  still runs on the owner\'s desktop computer.\n\nThis statement says, for each edition, what information the system\nholds, where it sits, when anything leaves the machine it sits on,\nand what choices the people involved have. Words we cannot avoid\nare explained the first time they appear.\n\n## 2. The desktop edition: your work stays on your computer\n\n### Where your work is stored\n\nEverything the desktop edition holds — the research database, the\ncorpora and files you load, the claims, anchor sets, jobs, and\nreports you create, and the program\'s settings — is stored in one\ndata folder on your own computer (on Windows, under your local\napplication data, in `GlossaLab\\data`). The database is a single\nfile in that folder. Nothing in the desktop edition stores your\nwork on anyone else\'s computer.\n\n### The install token\n\nThe program\'s interface and its data are guarded by one **token**:\na long random password the program creates for itself the first\ntime it runs. The token is kept in a small file inside the data\nfolder, set so only your operating-system account can read it\n(file mode 0600 on systems that support it). Anything that talks\nto the program — the app window, the tray, the command-line tools —\nmust present this token; anything that does not gets a refusal.\nThe token is never sent to us or to anyone else, it is never shown\nin full on any screen (screens show only a short fingerprint, the\nfirst characters of its checksum), and it can be replaced at any\ntime with the program\'s rotate command, which takes effect\nimmediately. A short health readout is the only thing the program\nanswers without the token, and it contains no research data.\n\n### Keys you type into Settings\n\nYou can give the program keys for outside services — for example an\nAI provider such as Mistral, OpenAI, Anthropic, or Google, scholarly\nsearch services, or an email account for notifications. Those keys\nare stored in a file named `.keys.json` inside the data folder,\nseparate from the database. Two honest details:\n\n- The file is **not encrypted**. It is protected by the same\n  restricted file permissions as the token file, and by your\n  operating-system account — anyone who can read your files as you\n  can read this file.\n- The program never shows a stored key back. Its settings screens\n  report only whether a key is set, never the key itself.\n\nLeaving a key out is always an option: the parts of the program\nthat need a key simply stay off, and nothing fails because a key is\nmissing.\n\n### The only times anything leaves your computer\n\nThe desktop edition has no reporting of its own — no usage\nstatistics, no crash reports, no check-ins with us. Data leaves\nyour computer only when you use a feature that needs an outside\nservice, and only to that service:\n\n- **AI features.** When you run a feature that asks an AI service\n  to read or draft text, the text you submit is sent to the\n  provider you configured, under your own account and key, and the\n  provider\'s own terms govern what it does with it.\n- **Scholarly sources.** The program searches and fetches public\n  research sources (for example Crossref, CORE, and GDELT) and\n  downloads what you ask it to. Your search terms go to those\n  services as part of the search.\n- **Email you configure.** If you set up the email settings, the\n  program sends the notices you asked for — a discovery digest and\n  completion notices for studies and experiments — from your mail\n  account to the addresses you configured. If the email settings\n  are blank, it sends nothing.\n\n### Copies and backups\n\nAny copy of your data folder or database — a backup, a snapshot,\nan export — exists only because you or your tools made it, and it\nlives wherever you put it. The program makes no copies of your\nwork anywhere else.\n\n## 3. The cloud edition: a members-only door to the same desktop\n\nThe cloud edition does not move the program. The research data\nstays on the owner\'s desktop computer, in the same install the\nowner uses locally. The website is a gate and a passageway in\nfront of it, run on Cloudflare (a hosting provider). Only people\nwith an account may sign in and use it; there is no public access\nand no self-service sign-up — every account begins as an invitation\nfrom an administrator, or as the single administrator account\ncreated when the deployment was set up.\n\n### What the website holds\n\nThe website\'s own database (Cloudflare D1) holds **identity data\nonly**:\n\n- **Accounts**: a username, an email address if one is on record,\n  the account\'s global type (owner, admin, member, or guest), its\n  status, and when it was created, disabled, or removed.\n- **Password hashes** — see below.\n- **Invitations**: who created the invitation, the email address it\n  was issued to (when it is bound to one), the workspace access it\n  carries, and its expiry, revocation, and redemption state.\n- **Sessions**: a record for each sign-in, with its creation, last\n  activity, and expiry times.\n- **Workspaces and access**: the list of workspaces (the program\'s\n  projects, carried under that name) and, for each person, the role\n  they hold in each workspace.\n- **Setup state**: whether the one-time administrator setup has\n  been completed.\n- **The audit log** — see below.\n\nThat is the whole list. **The research dataset is never copied to\nthe website\'s storage, or to any other cloud storage.** There is\nno cloud copy to browse, back up, or leak. When the desktop\ncomputer is off or the program is stopped, the website answers\nwith a plain "the host is offline" message and nothing else — it\nhas no project data of its own to serve.\n\n### Passwords and one-time codes\n\n- Passwords are stored only as **hashes** — a one-way scrambled\n  form (produced with Argon2id, a password-hashing method designed\n  to be slow to guess) from which the password cannot be read back.\n  Nobody, including an administrator, can see your password.\n- Invitation codes, session tokens, and the one-time setup code\n  are likewise stored only as hashes (SHA-256). The readable code\n  exists only in the invitation itself and in your browser.\n- There is **no password recovery by email** in this edition. If\n  you lose your password, an administrator disables the account\n  and issues a fresh invitation; that is the designed way back in.\n\n### Staying signed in\n\nSigning in sets one cookie in your browser (a small marker the\nwebsite uses to recognize your session). It is marked so scripts\ncannot read it and so it travels only over encrypted connections,\nand it lasts up to 30 days. You can see and revoke your sessions\nfrom your account page. Changing your password ends every other\nsession and keeps only the one you are using. An administrator\ncan revoke a session or disable an account at any time, and a\ndisabled account cannot sign in.\n\n### The audit log\n\nThe website keeps an **audit log**: an append-only list of\nsecurity-relevant events — sign-ins and failed sign-ins,\ninvitations created, redeemed, and revoked, password changes,\nsession revocations, account disabling and removal, and the\ncompletion of the one-time setup. Append-only is enforced by the\ndatabase itself, not just by convention: the log\'s rows cannot be\nedited or deleted through the database. The log records *that*\nthe setup was completed — never the setup code, and never any\npassword.\n\nIf an account is removed, the account is closed and its sessions\nare deleted, but the person\'s name stays attached to the work\nthey already did and to their past entries in the audit log. That\nis deliberate: the record of who did what is part of the research\nrecord.\n\n### How long identity records are kept\n\nExpired invitations and audit-log entries are retained for\n12 months. That period is the owner\'s policy default, set when\nthis statement was approved, and the owner can change it by a\nrecorded decision. It is a policy, not an automated process:\nnothing in the current build removes old entries on its own, and\nthe audit log\'s append-only construction means applying the\nperiod to audit entries is an operator act under the owner\'s\ndirection.\n\n### Workspaces: who can see what\n\nWork is organized into workspaces, and access is decided **per\nworkspace**. A person can hold a different role — owner, admin,\nmember, or viewer — in each workspace they belong to. Guests are\nnever more than viewers anywhere. You are shown only the\nworkspaces you belong to; a workspace you have no access to does\nnot appear in your lists, counts, or search results, not even as\na name. If you ask for something outside your access, the answer\nis the same "not found" you would get if it did not exist.\nA workspace that has been archived serves nothing to anyone.\n\n### How requests reach the desktop\n\nYour requests reach the program on the desktop only after you\nhave signed in, and only for the parts of the program your access\ncovers. The website removes any identity labels a visitor\'s\nbrowser attaches to a request and adds its own only after\nchecking your session, together with a per-deployment **service\ncredential** (a secret shared between the website and the desktop\nprogram) that proves the request really came through the website.\nThe desktop program\'s own install token (§2) plays no part here:\nthe website refuses it in every position, and it is never a\nbrowser credential. The connection between the website and the\ndesktop runs over a private tunnel whose secret lives only in a\nservice configuration on the desktop — never in the program\'s\ncode, records, or this statement.\n\nFeatures in the hosted app that call an AI service use the\nservice and key configured on the host desktop (§2). Text you\nsubmit to those features is therefore sent to that provider,\nunder the host\'s account.\n\n### Search engines\n\nThe hosted site is closed to search engines and indexing on\npurpose: its robots file tells crawlers to stay away from every\npage, and every response carries a "noindex" instruction. That\nposture stays until the owner decides to lift it.\n\n### The hosting provider\n\nThe gate described above runs on Cloudflare\'s network, so\nCloudflare necessarily handles the traffic between your browser\nand the desktop. What Cloudflare itself records in its own\noperational systems, and how long it keeps it, is governed by\nCloudflare\'s own terms and is not something this codebase\ncontrols or describes. The Worker\'s own configuration, which is\npart of this program\'s code, enables no logging or measurement\ncollection of its own; the one measurement service switched on\nfor this domain at the account level is Cloudflare Web\nAnalytics, described in §5.\n\n## 4. The research material itself\n\nThe program works on other scholars\' corpora, and those materials\ncarry their own rules, which the program follows:\n\n- The primary corpus (the Holdat Indus Corpus) is proprietary.\n  It is used for statistical analysis only. Its raw data is not\n  committed to the program\'s code repository and is never\n  released.\n- Files derived from the Corpus of Indus Seals and Inscriptions\n  (CISI) are reference material: used inside the research, not\n  for redistribution.\n- The program\'s custody rule, in its own words: **statistics\n  publishable, scans never redistributed.** Restricted material —\n  scans and what is derived from them — stays in restricted\n  folders on the owner\'s own machines. Through the cloud edition\n  it can be returned only inside a signed-in session, to a member\n  whose workspace access covers it, and under the same\n  restrictions the desktop application already enforces. Hosting\n  changes where the application runs; it does not widen what any\n  member is allowed to receive.\n\nWhat the program publishes is its own analysis: the preprint and\nits statistics (released under CC BY 4.0), and the public research\ntracker. The published readings are candidates, held to the\nprogram\'s standing position:\n\n> "The Indus script is not deciphered. The readings this program\n> records are candidate readings, each one a claim standing at a\n> stage of the claim lifecycle. A reading is not settled by being\n> recorded and checked, and no count in this program is a measure\n> of decipherment."\n\n## 5. What we do not do\n\n- We do not sell personal information, and no part of either\n  edition sends member or research data to advertisers or data\n  brokers.\n- The desktop edition contains no analytics or telemetry: it\n  reports nothing about your use to anyone (§2).\n- Web analytics **is** switched on for this domain at the hosting\n  account level. Cloudflare Web Analytics is enabled for\n  bitconcepts.tech with automatic installation, and its\n  configuration carries no exclusion for this site, so it\n  applies here as it does across the domain (checked against the\n  hosting account on 2026-11-05). It is Cloudflare\'s page-visit\n  measurement, run under Cloudflare\'s own terms; it measures\n  visits to pages and is not connected to the research dataset.\n\n## 6. Your controls\n\n- **Your account.** From your account page you can change your\n  email address and your password, and see and revoke your\n  sessions.\n- **Closing an account.** Ask an administrator to disable or\n  remove your account. Disabling stops sign-in immediately.\n  Removal closes the account and deletes its sessions; as §3\n  explains, your name remains on work you already did and in the\n  audit log.\n- **Exporting your work.** You can export your own work from a\n  workspace. A project exports as a single JSON bundle holding\n  the project and the work linked to it (hypotheses, notebooks,\n  citations, and correspondence). Reports you generate can be\n  downloaded as files, and corpus texts can be exported in text,\n  CSV, or JSON form. An export is a copy — it removes nothing —\n  and it reaches only the workspaces your access covers, under\n  the same rule as §3: a workspace you cannot see cannot be\n  exported by you.\n\n## 7. How this statement changes\n\nOnce approved, this text is kept as the single approved version\nunder the program\'s policy-sync rules. It changes only through a\nrecorded approval by the owner of the new text, and the copy the\nsystem serves is checked against the approved version\nautomatically, so the served words and the approved words cannot\nquietly drift apart.\n\n*Version: 1.0 — approved by the owner on 2026-11-05, effective\nfrom that date. This is the first approved version; the version\nhistory begins here.*\n\n## 8. Questions\n\nQuestions about this statement, or about how the program handles\nyour data, can be sent to tristen@bitconcepts.tech.'
